{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.GeomSum","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MonoidAlgebra.Support","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityWritheRibbon"],"declarations":14,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_bar_conjugate_sign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_bar_conjugate_sign","kind":"theorem","line":255,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L255-L271","doc":"**L2 sign corollary.**  With a unit sign `s ∈ {±1}` the duality constant\n`ε_λ = s^L` is itself a sign: the bar image of the channel-`λ` character is\n`ε_λ` times the conjugate-channel character, `ε_λ ∈ {±1}` (uniformly `−1` at\n`n=3`, `+1` at `n=6`, matching the probe's `(−1)^L`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_bar_conjugate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_bar_conjugate","kind":"theorem","line":226,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L226-L253","doc":"**L2 — bar-involution ↔ conjugation duality (conditional on the seminormal\nbar–conjugate relation).**\n\nThe Hecke **bar involution** `q ↦ q⁻¹` swaps the seminormal eigenvalues\n`q ↔ −q⁻¹`, which is exactly the intertwiner `V_λ ≅ V_{λ'}` to the\n**conjugate** Wedderburn channel `λ'` (transpose partition; `dim V_λ =\ndim V_{λ'}`, so the path index sets are in bijection `φ`).  At the level of\nmatrix entries this is a uniform sign `s ∈ {±1}` and a path bijection `φ` with\n\n  `a_λ(p,i)(q⁻¹) = s · a_{λ'}(φ p, i)(q)`  (the seminormal bar–conjugate input).\n\nFrom this **factor-level** categorical input the **character-level** duality\nfollows by reindexing along `φ` and extracting the `L` signs:\n\n  `χ_λ(β)(q⁻¹) = s^L · χ_{λ'}(β)(q)`.\n\nThis is the §3b-cond conditional-class shape: the bar–conjugate relation is the\nexplicit hypothesis; the duality is **derived**, not assumed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_support_parity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_support_parity","kind":"theorem","line":208,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L208-L222","doc":"**L1 — literal Laurent-support form.**  The Hecke character\n`χ_λ(β) = Σ_p Π_{i<L} a(p,i)`, a sum of `L`-fold products of odd (parity-`1`)\ncrossing factors, has **every Laurent exponent of parity `≡ L (mod 2)`**.  This\nis the exponent-level statement of `character_qneg_parity` (whose `q↦−q` form\n`χ(−q)=(−1)^L χ(q)` is the evaluation face of the same fact). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.listProd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.listProd","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L195-L206","doc":"A product of `L` odd (parity-`1`) Laurent factors is `(L mod 2)`-homogeneous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.add","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L187-L193","doc":"Sum of `ε`-homogeneous Laurent polynomials is `ε`-homogeneous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.zero","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L183-L185","doc":"The zero polynomial is vacuously parity-`ε` for any `ε`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.one","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L176-L181","doc":"The constant `1` is parity-`0` (its only exponent is `0`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom.mul","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L167-L174","doc":"Parity adds under multiplication of Laurent polynomials (the exponent of a\nproduct term is the sum of the factor exponents). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.ParityHom","kind":"def","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L160-L165","doc":"A Laurent polynomial `p ∈ ℝ[ℤ] = LaurentPolynomial ℝ` is **`ε`-parity-\nhomogeneous** when every exponent in its support has parity `ε` (the zero\npolynomial vacuously, for every `ε`).  This is the literal \"all exponents\n≡ ε (mod 2)\" content behind the `q ↦ −q` symmetry of `character_qneg_parity`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_qneg_parity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.character_qneg_parity","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L131-L156","doc":"**L1 — order-parity = word-length parity.**\n\nModel the Hecke character of a length-`L` braid word `β` in channel `λ` as a\nsum over the index paths of the seminormal matrix product,\n`χ_λ(β)(q) = Σ_p Π_{i<L} a(p,i)(q)`, where each factor `a(p,i)` is an entry of\none crossing matrix `σ_j` (or `σ_j − h·I`).  Each such factor is an **odd**\nLaurent polynomial: its `q ↦ −q` image is its negation (the seminormal\neigenvalues `q, −q⁻¹` and the off-diagonal entries all have odd Laurent\ndegree, since `q ↦ −q` sends `q^k ↦ (−1)^k q^k`).  Then\n\n  `χ_λ(β)(−q) = (−1)^L · χ_λ(β)(q)`,\n\nequivalently every exponent of the Laurent character has parity `≡ L (mod 2)`.\nThis makes the PR #2496 parity obstruction a theorem: a same-type-nucleon /\nwinding tower flips parity because the nucleon word has odd length `L = 11`,\nso `11·k` alternates parity in `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_gf_ribbon_class","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_gf_ribbon_class","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L116-L127","doc":"**L3 against the `RibbonTwistEigenvalue` class.**  The fullest discharge:\nwith an actual ribbon-eigenvalue instance `[RibbonTwistEigenvalue q]`, the\nframing tower built from the class's own `framing_eigenvalue` is the single-pole\nrational, pole at `t = (θ_λ)⁻¹`.  No new hypothesis — `framing_formula` supplies\nthe eigenvalue and `framing_gf_ribbon` the geometric closure. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_gf_ribbon","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_gf_ribbon","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L92-L114","doc":"**L3 discharged at the concrete ribbon eigenvalue `θ_λ = q^{2κ}`.**\n\nInstantiating `framing_gf_closed_form` at the framed character\n`c m = q^{2mκ}·χ₀` — the action of the `m`-fold full twist `Δ^{2m}` on `V_λ`\nthrough the ribbon eigenvalue `θ_λ = q^{2κ}`\n(`QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityWritheRibbon.RibbonTwistEigenvalue.framing_formula`,\nhere via `framing_eigenvalue_pow`).  The hypothesis `hstep` is **discharged**\n(not assumed): the one-step scaling `q^{2(m+1)κ} = q^{2κ}·q^{2mκ}` is proved.\nThe framing tower is then the single-pole rational with pole at `t = q^{−2κ}`:\n\n  `(1 − t·q^{2κ}) · Σ_{m<N} t^m · q^{2mκ} · χ₀ = χ₀ · (1 − (t·q^{2κ})^N)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_gf_closed_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_gf_closed_form","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L56-L90","doc":"**L3 — framing generating function (finite closed form).**\n\nLet `c m = χ_λ(β · Δ^{2m})` be the Hecke character of the braid `β` framed\nby `m` full twists, in Wedderburn channel `λ`.  Centrality of the full\ntwist `Δ² ∈ Z(H_n(q))` together with the ribbon eigenvalue\n`Δ²|_{V_λ} = θ` (`θ = q^{2c(λ)}`, `framing_eigenvalue_pow`) gives the\none-step scaling `c (m+1) = θ · c m`.  Then the framing generating function\nis the **single-pole rational** `c 0 / (1 − t·θ)`: the partial sums satisfy\nthe finite geometric closed form\n\n  `(1 − t·θ) · Σ_{m<N} t^m · c m = c 0 · (1 − (t·θ)^N)`,\n\nso the only pole is at `t = θ⁻¹ = q^{−2c(λ)}`.  The result is **not**\nvacuous: it pins the framing tower as geometric with ratio `t·θ` and\nleading coefficient `c 0`, which is the substance of the eigenbasis\nreframing (the framing tower separates orders by content). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_eigenvalue_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.framing_eigenvalue_pow","kind":"theorem","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/EigenbasisStructuralLemmas.lean#L43-L54","doc":"**Ribbon power law.**  The eigenvalue of the `m`-fold full twist\n`Δ^{2m}` on the channel `V_λ` is the `m`-th power of the single-twist\neigenvalue `θ_λ = q^{2κ_λ}`:\n\n  `q^{2mκ} = (q^{2κ})^m`.\n\nThis is the algebraic content linking the `m`-fold framing to the ribbon\neigenvalue; it holds for every real base (the `zpow` identity is valid in a\n`GroupWithZero`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}